@inject AppSession Session
@inject PaletteSwitch Palette
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@implements IDisposable

@* The five destinations that carry the loop the app is built around: record what you own, check
   what every set still needs, log what you opened, and see which pack. Wonder Pick is a separate
   in-game mechanic and sits behind More with everything else you go to occasionally.

   THE ORDER IS THE LOOP, AND PROGRESS COMES BEFORE PACKS
   ==================================================================================
   Progress sits second and Packs fourth, which is the reverse of how they went in. Progress is
   where you arrive with "what am I missing", and every panel on it links out to Packs for one
   set -- so it is the hub and Packs is what it hands you off to. A hub after the page it feeds
   reads as a detour.

   Chase Lists is the destination this displaced. A chase list is something you build by hand for
   cards outside your plan -- worth having, reached occasionally -- while every set's standing
   against the target is checked as often as which pack to open. Chases is in More, in the palette,
   and in the scope picker on four pages, so nothing about it got harder to reach except by a tap.

   Log keeps the centre either way: it is the only tab that writes something, and the swap above
   moves the two either side of it rather than moving it.

   Hiding the rest is legitimate under the rule the UI follows everywhere: hide controls freely,
   never hide active state that changes what the numbers mean. A destination is not a claim about
   data. *@
<nav class="tab-bar" aria-label="Main">
    @* A plain anchor, and the one entry in this bar that is not a NavLink.
       ==================================================================================
       The collection answers to two URLs -- the root and /collection -- and NavLink matches one
       href, so it cannot be active at both. See Routes.IsCollection for what that cost: the root
       rendered the collection with nothing lit up in either nav. Computing this one keeps a single
       source of truth for it, where adding `active` alongside a NavLink would have two things
       deciding the same class. *@
    <a class="tab @(AtCollection ? "active" : null)" href="collection">
        <span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">
            <NavIcon For="collection" />
        </span>
        <span class="lbl">Collection</span>
    </a>

    <NavLink class="tab" href="progress">
        <span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">
            <NavIcon For="progress" />
        </span>
        <span class="lbl">Progress</span>
    </NavLink>

    @* Centred and larger: the only tab that writes something, and the one repeated many times a
       day. The others are places; this is the action.

       The pack rather than a plus, and the same drawing as the wordmark's. A plus said "this one
       writes something" without saying what; the pack says what is being logged. It is white here
       because the filled circle sets `color: #fff` and the mark paints in currentColor, so nothing
       about the glyph changes between its two homes.

       No label under it, unlike the four destinations: the circle is big enough to carry the
       meaning on its own, and dropping the word buys the height the larger circle needs. The name
       still reaches a screen reader through aria-label. *@
    <NavLink class="tab tab-primary" href="log" aria-label="Log a pack">
        <span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">
            <PackMark Size="32" />
        </span>
    </NavLink>

    <NavLink class="tab" href="packs">
        <span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">
            <NavIcon For="packs" />
        </span>
        <span class="lbl">Packs</span>
    </NavLink>

    @* A native popover: top layer, light dismiss and Escape come free, and unlike a dialog it is
       non-modal, which is right for a menu. *@
    <button class="tab" popovertarget="nav-more" aria-label="More">
        <span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">
            <NavIcon For="more" />
        </span>
        <span class="lbl">More</span>
    </button>
</nav>

<MoreMenu Id="nav-more" />

@code {
    /// <summary>Whether the collection is the page we are on, at either of its two URLs.</summary>
    private bool AtCollection => Routes.IsCollection(Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri));

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Session.Changed += OnChanged;

        // See the note on the collection tab: its active state is computed during render, so it
        // needs telling when the URL moves. The NavLinks beside it do that for themselves.
        Nav.LocationChanged += OnLocationChanged;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Session.Changed -= OnChanged;
        Nav.LocationChanged -= OnLocationChanged;
    }

    private void OnLocationChanged(object? sender, LocationChangedEventArgs e) =>
        InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    private void OnChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
}
